**Definition 1.2 (specific missing relation).** At an actual live cut after
an unknown original history, an observer is asked to acquire inputs that
support the next lawful operation and its complete successor. The negative
contract here supplies only the sole surviving specimen and common metadata
at that cut. It permits arbitrary common specimen-only processing and
retention, with every acquisition branch counted. It supplies no readable
old generator word, source callback, height programme, parity helper,
waveform log, environment port or additional specimen.

The quantitative conclusions have three different meanings:

| Consumer | Conclusion and exact sharpness boundary |
| --- | --- |
| One horizontal request over an interval of possible heights | A general affine survival lower bound, sharp among unrestricted affine scalar approximations. |
| One horizontal request on twelve actual histories | An exact discrepancy between two full ideal output mixtures, giving live error at least $1/22$ and complete-experiment error at least $1/99$. Neither is asserted to be the full-instrument minimax value. |
| One original return excursion at two possible heights | Exact specimen-only survival-bit minimax error $23/434$. Its attaining effect does not reproduce the instrument. |

The fixed-acceptance decision calculation and the conditional return-record
consumer in §§9–10 identify the same missing acquisition/actuation relation.
They neither obtain a complete observer nor assert that exact height labels
are the only possible additional resource.

